# Project parity review remediation — 2026-10-27

- Plan: `plan/2026/10/02-project-parity-review.md` and its existing Traditional Chinese companion; reviewed against Java client 5.5.1.
- Branch: `fix/parity-review-2026-10-27`, created from `a91e91bcdcfe92b01a351c56c7ce0dd4ff393e96`.
- User constraint: review plans may be committed; keep all files under `plan/2026/10/02-project-parity-review-evidence/` out of commits.
- Repository instructions: no applicable `AGENTS.md` found in this repository. Applied the `coding` skill's phased workflow and append-only execution log.

## Phase 1 — message integrity and Lite initial offsets

- Changed compatibility decoder defaults to unbounded limits, retaining explicit internal limits for callers that opt into them. This preserves all valid Java-sized records and batches instead of returning a successful truncated prefix.
- Added a Common regression for 24 compressed 3 MiB records and one compressed 65 MiB body; added PullAPIWrapper coverage asserting the 24 messages remain delivered with the Broker's `nextBeginOffset` unchanged.
- Corrected Lite initial-offset selection for stored valid, missing, and unavailable offsets, retry-topic LAST/TIMESTAMP cases, and negative SEARCH_OFFSET results.
- Added Lite policy-matrix coverage for the supported LAST, FIRST, and TIMESTAMP modes, retry/regular topics, and invalid timestamps.
- The first Lite matrix run incorrectly marked stored offset `0` as missing; Java uses exactly `-1` for missing. Corrected the case inputs and reran successfully.
- Focused checks passed: common decoder oversized-compressed-batch regression; PullAPIWrapper 24-record delivery regression; Lite initial-offset matrix; external Java/Rust probe. The probe now reports one 65 MiB compressed message decoded and delivered, and all 24 3 MiB messages delivered with `next_begin_offset=24`; Java reported the same counts and body sizes. Probe output retained at `/tmp/rocketmq-parity-review-remediated-phase1.json`.
- `cargo fmt --all` and `git diff --check` passed. Commit: pending.

## Phase 2 — POP control flow and lifecycle

- POP orderly batches now stop before listener invocation, consume hooks, ACK, or visibility updates, matching the pinned Java `ConsumeRequest.run` path. An offline Java invocation confirmed zero orderly listener calls and an unchanged wait-ACK count; Rust regression checks the listener, hooks, and side-effect counters.
- POP and direct-consume listener tasks now use the existing `AbortOnDrop` ownership pattern. The POP shutdown regression waits for a blocked listener's drop signal, releases its barrier after shutdown, and verifies no late side effect; direct-consume cancellation has the same drop-sentinel check.
- POP shutdown now polls all worker handles against one service deadline, aborts remaining workers together, and observes the cancellation phase against one second shared deadline. Paused-time coverage verifies 1 and 20 pending workers stay within the same two-phase 200 ms budget.
- To complete R01 call-site coverage, added Lite fetched-offset acceptance, compressed POP response, and compressed Admin query regressions; each verifies all 24 valid 3 MiB messages remain available.
- One initial POP lifecycle assertion expected the wait-ACK counter to be zero after cancellation. Inspection showed Java/Rust counters track outstanding messages until ACK, so the test now verifies the count remains outstanding while the listener is dropped and no callback can produce a late ACK.
- `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --locked` passed (277 unit tests plus all package integration tests and doctests). Focused Core compressed-query test passed. The updated Java/Rust offline probe passed and reported `java_pop_orderly_listener_calls=0, wait_ack_count=1`; output retained at `/tmp/rocketmq-parity-review-remediated-phase2.json`.
- `cargo fmt --all` passed. Commit: pending.
